---
/**
 * /senate/responses/ — how the government answered, when it answered.
 *
 * A dynamic route rather than a plain page so that getStaticPaths can return
 * nothing before launch. A page file would be emitted whether or not anything
 * linked to it; this way there is no HTML on the server until LAUNCHED is true.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  closureDetail, recommendationFloor, responseDoc, history, humanDuration, fmt, formatDate,
  coverage,
} from "../../lib/data";
import { FORMS, formHref } from "../../lib/forms";
import PositionsBar from "../../components/PositionsBar.astro";
import { LAUNCHED } from "../../lib/launch";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LAUNCHED ? [{ params: { page: "responses" } }] : [];
}

const c = closureDetail();
/* Whether the response stated a position, recommendation by recommendation.
   Null only on a dataset built before coverage.py existed. */
const cov = coverage();
const pct = (x: number | null) => (x === null ? "—" : `${(x * 100).toFixed(0)}%`);
/**
 * The page used to lead with the count of "the Government notes this
 * recommendation" sentences, presented as a count of recommendations. It is
 * not one. Forty-five of the closures never use the sentence, some use it once
 * for a report with twenty-eight recommendations, and the committee's own
 * count — the authoritative one — cannot be reached for the corpus. The
 * headline is now the reports, which is exact, and the recommendation figure
 * appears lower down as the floor it is.
 */
const floor = recommendationFloor();
const observed = history().filter((p) => p.basis === "observed").at(-1);
const asAt = formatDate(observed?.asAt ?? new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10));
const slowest = c.slowest!;
// The anchor document. Counts come from the dataset, never typed in.
const anchor = responseDoc("15895");
const peakYear = c.byYear.filter((y) => y.closures > 0)
  .reduce((a, b) => (!a || b.share > a.share ? b : a), null as (typeof c.byYear)[number] | null);
---

<Base
  title="How the government answered"
  description="Since mid-2022 the government has closed hundreds of committee reports with a template sentence that states no position on any recommendation. This is the count, the pattern, and the documents."
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Government responses · both chambers · as at {asAt}</p>
    <h1 class="claim"><span class="figure">{fmt(c.proforma)}</span> government responses that closed a committee report without stating a position on any recommendation.</h1>
    <p class="lede measure">
      A report leaves the register when a response is tabled. It does not follow that the
      report was answered. Of the {fmt(c.corpus)} government responses tabled since mid-2022,
      {" "}<strong>{fmt(c.proforma)}</strong> disposed of the committee’s recommendations with a
      repeated template sentence, that given the passage of time a substantive response is no
      longer appropriate, and took a position on none of them. Nothing accepted, nothing
      rejected, nothing qualified.
    </p>
    <p class="lede measure">
      That sentence is used <strong>{fmt(floor.templateUses)}</strong> times across those
      {" "}{fmt(floor.closures)} documents, counting every form of it. A stricter count, of
      the “notes this recommendation” form alone, gives {fmt(floor.sentences)}, and that is
      the one the floor below is built from. How many individual recommendations the sentence
      disposed of is a harder question, and the answer below is a floor, not an estimate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>The slowest response on the Senate’s record</h2>
    <p>
      The Senate select committee’s report <em>{slowest.inquiry}</em> was tabled on
      {" "}{formatDate(slowest.reportTabled)}. The government responded on
      {" "}{formatDate(slowest.responseTabled)}, {fmt(slowest.days)} days later, or
      {" "}{humanDuration(slowest.days)}. It is the longest gap between a report and a
      response anywhere in the Senate’s register.
    </p>
    <p>
      The response runs to {anchor.recommendationsNoted} entries: {anchor.recommendationsNoted - 1}
      {" "}for the committee’s own recommendations and one under the document’s own final
      heading, “Minority/dissenting recommendations”. Against every one of them it
      records that the government notes the recommendation and that, given the passage of
      time, a substantive response is no longer appropriate: the same sentence,
      {" "}{anchor.templateHits} times.
      {" "}{anchor.acceptances === 0 ? "Nothing is accepted, rejected or qualified." : ""}
      {" "}<a href={anchor.url}>Read it</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>How many recommendations that is, and why the number is a floor</h2>
    <p>
      The count of reports is exact. The count of recommendations is not, and this site will
      not print one as though it were. The authoritative number of recommendations a
      committee made is in the committee’s report, and for this corpus those reports cannot
      be reached: the Tabled Documents system’s own report-to-response links reach one of the{" "}
      {fmt(floor.closures)} closures, and a title search of that system finds none of the
      others, because the reports they closed were tabled before its index begins.
    </p>
    <p>
      What each response shows on its own pages is a different matter. Where a closure sets out
      the recommendations numbered one to N with no gaps, quotes each one, and answers each
      with the template sentence exactly once, the arithmetic is visible on the page.
      {" "}<strong>{fmt(floor.counted)} of the {fmt(floor.closures)} closures do that, and they
      hold {fmt(floor.recommendations)} recommendations.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      So: <strong>at least {fmt(floor.recommendations)} recommendations</strong> were disposed
      of by a sentence that took a position on none of them. At least, because the other{" "}
      {fmt(floor.closures - floor.counted)} closures are excluded, not estimated:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="form-letter-closures-not-counted-caption" tabindex="0">
    <table class="register narrow">
      <caption class="sr-only" id="form-letter-closures-not-counted-caption">Form-letter closures not counted in the recommendation floor, by reason</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Not counted, because</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Documents</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {floor.reasons.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.why}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(r.count)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The largest of those groups is the one that matters most: those documents close a report
      without naming a single recommendation, often in two or three sentences. Whatever they
      disposed of is not in the {fmt(floor.recommendations)}. The two-recommendation minimum
      exists because a response laid out as a table carries no “Recommendation N” heading at
      all, and one document that met every other test with a single recommendation turned out
      to have at least five. Every closure, its numbers and the reason it is or is not counted
      are in <code>recommendation_counts.csv</code> in the
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket">public dataset</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>It is not routine practice. It comes in bursts.</h2>
    <p>
      Form-letter closures are not spread evenly across the years. There were none at all in
      the first two years of the corpus, and then two clearing events.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="form-letter-closures-by-year-caption" tabindex="0">
    <table class="register narrow">
      <caption class="sr-only" id="form-letter-closures-by-year-caption">Form-letter closures by year tabled</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Year tabled</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Responses</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Form-letter closures</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Share</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {c.byYear.map((y) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{y.year}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(y.responses)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(y.closures)}</td>
            <td class="num">{y.closures ? `${(y.share * 100).toFixed(1)}%` : "—"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      {peakYear && `In ${peakYear.year}, ${(peakYear.share * 100).toFixed(0)} per cent of all government responses tabled were form-letter closures.`}
      {" "}A backlog cleared this way falls just as fast on a chart as one cleared by answering.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>Four ways to answer without answering</h2>
    <p>
      The count above uses one test, and it is a narrow one: a repeated template sentence.
      That is the most conspicuous way of closing a report without answering it, and it is
      not the only way. Three more forms are named below. None is detected automatically,
      each is illustrated by a document anyone can download, and every one of them would be
      counted as a substantive response by the number at the top of this page.
    </p>
    <ol class="forms">
      {FORMS.map((f) => (
        <li>
          <p class="form-name">
            {f.name}
            <span class={f.detected ? "tag on" : "tag"}>{f.detected ? "counted above" : "not counted"}</span>
          </p>
          <p class="form-what">{f.what}</p>
          <p class="note"><a href={formHref(f)}>{f.example}</a>. {f.exampleNote}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p>
      So {fmt(c.proforma)} is a floor. The true figure for “answered nothing” is higher, and
      reaching it means asking, for every recommendation a committee made, whether the response
      stated a position on it. That count is below.
      {" "}<a href="/methods/#closures">How this is classified, and why the other three are not</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  {cov && (
    <section class="wrap measure" id="coverage">
      <h2>Whether a position was stated, recommendation by recommendation</h2>
      <p>
        For each recommendation this site can show, the question is whether the response
        stated a position on it. A position is a verdict word used of the recommendation:
        supported, agreed, accepted, endorsed, rejected, declined, not supported, not agreed,
        not accepted or implemented, in full, in part or in principle. “The Government notes
        this recommendation” followed by a description of existing policy is not a position.
        The test is mechanical, and every result on the
        {" "}<a href="/recommendations/">search page</a> carries its label beside the
        government’s words, so the label can be checked against them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Of the <strong>{fmt(cov.total.recommendations)}</strong> recommendations in
        {" "}{fmt(cov.total.responses)} responses, <strong>{fmt(cov.total.positionStated)}</strong>
        {" "}({pct(cov.total.coverage)}) received a stated position. {fmt(cov.total.notedNoPosition)} were
        noted without one, {fmt(cov.total.formLetter)} were closed with the form letter, and
        {" "}{fmt(cov.total.notAddressedIndividually)} were passed over by a response that answered the
        report as a whole. {fmt(cov.total.responsesWithNoPositionAtAll)} of the
        {" "}{fmt(cov.total.responses)} responses stated a position on none of their recommendations;
        {" "}{fmt(cov.total.responsesFullyCovered)} stated one on every recommendation.
      </p>
      <h3>What the positions were</h3>
      <p>
        Where a position was stated, the government’s own verdict word is sorted three ways and
        nothing is inferred from it: <em>accepted</em> (supported, agreed, accepted, endorsed or
        implemented, unqualified); <em>in part or in principle</em> (the same sentence qualifies
        it); <em>not accepted</em> (negated, or rejected, declined, disagreed). “Supports in
        principle” therefore counts as in part or in principle, whatever it goes on to promise.
      </p>
      <PositionsBar total={cov.total} id="positions" />
      <details class="tv">
      <summary>Table view: recommendations by what the response said</summary>
      <div class="scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="recommendations-by-what-the-caption" tabindex="0">
      <table class="register narrow">
        <caption class="sr-only" id="recommendations-by-what-the-caption">Recommendations by what the response said about them</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">What the response said</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Recommendations</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Share of all shown</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ["Accepted, supported or agreed", cov.total.accepted],
            ["In part or in principle", cov.total.inPartOrInPrinciple],
            ["Not accepted", cov.total.notAccepted],
            ["Noted, no position stated", cov.total.notedNoPosition],
            ["Closed with the form letter", cov.total.formLetter],
            ["Not addressed individually", cov.total.notAddressedIndividually],
          ].map(([label, n]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{label}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(n as number)}</td>
              <td class="num">{cov.total.recommendations ? `${(((n as number) / cov.total.recommendations) * 100).toFixed(0)}%` : "—"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      </details>
      <h3>By year</h3>
      <div class="scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="stated-positions-by-year-caption" tabindex="0">
      <table class="register narrow">
        <caption class="sr-only" id="stated-positions-by-year-caption">Stated positions by year the response was tabled</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Year tabled</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Responses</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Recommendations</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Position stated</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Share</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Accepted</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">In part or in principle</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Not accepted</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {cov.byYear.map(({ year, bucket }) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{year}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(bucket.responses)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(bucket.recommendations)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(bucket.positionStated)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(bucket.coverage)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(bucket.accepted)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(bucket.inPartOrInPrinciple)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(bucket.notAccepted)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Excluded, and why. {fmt(cov.responsesWithNothingIndexed)} of the {fmt(cov.responsesInCorpus)} responses
        in the corpus hold nothing the index can read (no numbered recommendations in the response and
        no readable report), so they are not in these figures. {fmt(cov.total.unreadable)} recommendations
        whose government words could not be separated from the recommendation’s own text are counted on
        neither side. {fmt(cov.dissentingExcluded)} dissenting, minority or party recommendations are left
        out, because the government owes the committee a response, not the minority. The corpus is every
        response since mid-2022, all but two of them tabled under one government, so this figure cannot
        compare governments. A response that commits to action without a verdict word (“the Government
        will legislate this”) is counted as noted, because the test cannot see it; “position stated” is
        therefore a floor and “noted” a ceiling. Every response, with its counts, is in
        {" "}<code>coverage.csv</code> in the
        {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket">public dataset</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <p class="note">
      Corpus: {fmt(c.corpus)} responses tabled since mid-2022, after excluding
      {" "}{fmt(c.excluded)} documents that answer somebody other than a parliamentary
      committee. Every document, its classification and the exclusion reasons are in the
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket">public dataset</a>,
      licensed CC BY 4.0. If you believe a document is misclassified, write to
      {" "}<a href="mailto:corrections@toohardbasket.org.au">corrections@toohardbasket.org.au</a>;
      corrections are listed in the <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 2rem; }
  .figure {
    display: block;
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 2rem + 8vw, 6.5rem);
    line-height: 0.95; font-weight: 650; letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .claim {
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1rem + 1vw, 1.6rem); font-weight: 500;
    max-width: 26ch; margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.75rem; }
  .note { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-secondary); }
  table.register { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  table.register td { padding-right: 1rem; }
  table.narrow { max-width: 44rem; }
  .tv { margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .tv summary { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-secondary); cursor: pointer; }

  ol.forms { list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.4rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  ol.forms li { margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
  ol.forms p { margin: 0 0 0.25rem; }
  .form-name { font-weight: 600; }
  .form-what { color: var(--ink-secondary); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .tag {
    display: inline-block; margin-left: 0.5rem; padding: 0.05rem 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 2px; vertical-align: 0.1em;
  }
  .tag.on { color: var(--series-1); border-color: var(--series-1); }
</style>
